{"vid":"4mg_x8yndyQ","source":"4mg_x8yndyQ.en-en.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":14.9,"txt":"Hi, Tim here with another 925 English lesson."},{"t":21.4,"txt":"In today’s lesson, we’re going to learn how to express doubt in English."},{"t":28.9,"txt":"Have you ever been in a meeting when someone says something and you think “hmm… I’m not"},{"t":37.2,"txt":"really sure about that.” You might be unsure about whether it’s true, or whether it’s a good idea."},{"t":48,"txt":"We call that feeling “doubt.”"},{"t":51.7,"txt":"And it’s important that you are able to express doubt confidently and politely."},{"t":59.8,"txt":"Now, if you’re a little bit unsure of something, you can use the expression I just used, and say"},{"t":68.7,"txt":"something like “I’m not really sure” or “I’m not 100% sure.” Or you could say very directly “well,"},{"t":80.8,"txt":"I have a few doubts.” Another way to express doubt is with a question, like “Are you certain that"},{"t":90.8,"txt":"would work?” Just by asking the question, you are telling people you have doubts."},{"t":98.3,"txt":"Let’s try a bit of practice with some of these ways of expressing mild doubt."},{"t":105,"txt":"Listen to each example, then repeat it yourself. Ready? Let’s get started."},{"t":113.2,"txt":"I’m not 100% sure about that."},{"t":122.6,"txt":"Hmm… well, I have a few doubts."},{"t":130.7,"txt":"That seems a bit unlikely to me."},{"t":139.2,"txt":"Are you certain that would work?"},{"t":146.4,"txt":"You might notice that these expressions sound more polite than just saying “I doubt that.” The reason"},{"t":157,"txt":"for this is softening words like “a few” or “a bit” and hesitation words like “hmm” and “well.”"},{"t":172.4,"txt":"But sometimes you may be less concerned about being polite. Instead, you might want to show"},{"t":181,"txt":"strong doubt. And to do that, we can be more direct. For example, you could say"},{"t":190.7,"txt":"“there’s no way that would work.” That certainly sounds clear and direct, doesn’t it?"},{"t":199.5,"txt":"You could also say “that’s simply not feasible,” which means it won’t work."},{"t":207.2,"txt":"Or “I really don’t think that’s a possibility.”"},{"t":211.7,"txt":"These are all ways to show you have strong doubts about what’s being talked about."},{"t":219.2,"txt":"Let’s try some examples of showing strong doubts. Remember to repeat the"},{"t":225.5,"txt":"examples after you hear them."},{"t":228.8,"txt":"There’s no way that would work."},{"t":236,"txt":"I’m sorry but that’s simply not feasible."},{"t":244.3,"txt":"I seriously doubt it."},{"t":249.2,"txt":"I really don’t think that’s a possibility at all."},{"t":259.9,"txt":"These are strong and direct expressions. But you did hear one common way to make"},{"t":266.9,"txt":"them a bit more polite: the expression “I’m sorry but.” You can use this to introduce a"},{"t":276.5,"txt":"strong or honest opinion, such as “I’m sorry but that’s really not possible.”"},{"t":284.8,"txt":"Now, what if you don’t have strong doubts, and you’re simply unsure which of two competing ideas"},{"t":293.5,"txt":"or positions you support? Well, in this case, you might want to take a “neutral” position."},{"t":302.8,"txt":"If you’re neutral, it means you haven’t decided yet or you don’t want to commit to one side."},{"t":311.5,"txt":"In this case, you might say “I could go either way” or “I can see both sides of this issue.”"},{"t":321.8,"txt":"Another way to express this idea is with the idiom “I’m on the fence about it.”"},{"t":329,"txt":"That simply means you’re not on one side or the other, but right in the middle."},{"t":336.6,"txt":"How about some practice with these expressions? Once again, repeat the"},{"t":343.1,"txt":"examples after you hear them."},{"t":347.2,"txt":"I could go either way on this."},{"t":348,"txt":"ready to make a decision yet because you need more information or the future is uncertain."},{"t":355.1,"txt":"I’m kind of on the fence about it."},{"t":363.5,"txt":"I guess we’ll just have to wait and see."},{"t":371.2,"txt":"Yeah, well, I can see two sides to this issue."},{"t":380.6,"txt":"Did you hear that expression “we’ll just have to wait and see?” That’s simply a way of saying you’re not"},{"t":397.7,"txt":"All right, we’ve practiced different ways of expressing doubt."},{"t":402.9,"txt":"But how does this sound in a conversation? Let’s listen to a short dialog between Nick and Kendra."},{"t":412.1,"txt":"Nick is suggesting ways of reducing costs, and Kendra is expressing doubt."},{"t":421.4,"txt":"Nick: One way to reduce costs is to get a smaller office."},{"t":426,"txt":"Kendra: I’m not 100% sure about that."},{"t":429,"txt":"Nick: Well, we could cut staff instead."},{"t":432.6,"txt":"Kendra: I’m sorry but that’s simply not feasible."},{"t":438.1,"txt":"Now it’s your turn to practice. We’ll repeat the dialog, but this time we’re going to beep out"},{"t":445.7,"txt":"the second speaker’s words. You will have to say those parts yourself. Remember to start by saying"},{"t":453.9,"txt":"you’re not 100% sure about the idea. Then say you think Nick’s second idea simply isn’t feasible."},{"t":465,"txt":"Ready? Here we go!"},{"t":469.9,"txt":"One way to reduce costs is to get a smaller office."},{"t":480.2,"txt":"Well, we could cut staff instead."},{"t":490.1,"txt":"Okay, that’s all for this lesson! We’ve learned how to express mild and strong doubt, as well"},{"t":499.2,"txt":"as how to take a neutral position. We’ll be back soon with some more useful English expressions."},{"t":508.3,"txt":"Until then, so long and happy learning!"}],"words":[{"t":14.94,"w":"Hi,"},{"t":14.94,"w":"Tim"},{"t":14.94,"w":"here"},{"t":14.94,"w":"with"},{"t":14.94,"w":"another"},{"t":14.94,"w":"925"},{"t":14.94,"w":"English"},{"t":14.94,"w":"lesson."},{"t":21.42,"w":"In"},{"t":21.42,"w":"today’s"},{"t":21.42,"w":"lesson,"},{"t":21.42,"w":"we’re"},{"t":21.42,"w":"going"},{"t":21.42,"w":"to"},{"t":21.42,"w":"learn"},{"t":21.42,"w":"how"},{"t":21.42,"w":"to"},{"t":21.42,"w":"ex